    (H := H) S hreg A htrans μ x O hO hxO

end NormalizedFiniteOrbitArea


/-! ## 280. Updated normalized-area boundary

The finite-orbit multiplicity has now been removed at the level of actual measures.  The raw orbit
pushforward is weighted by the reciprocal intrinsic orbit degree using `Measure.withDensity`; on
every refined quotient chart this normalized measure restricts exactly to the pushforward of one
upstairs sheet.  Transport through the canonical regular-orbit homeomorphism preserves that exact
identity on the actual reduced regular locus.  Consequently the repaired physical full charts of
Section 273 can now descend with unit Lebesgue density, without any source/target degree comparison.

The remaining determinant-one work is chart assembly rather than normalization: instantiate
Advance 10 with the repaired bounded full atlas, shrink each refined sheet inside an interior or
bounded-edge physical chart, identify the resulting descended reduced coordinate measure with
Lebesgue using Section 273, and feed the resulting local affine scaling equalities into Section 259's
`scalesFlatArea_of_countable_local_restrictions`.  Finite/nonzero mass of the normalized measure must
also be recorded before packaging `HasFiniteFlatAreaScaling`.
-/

/-! ## 281. Finite and nonzero total mass of the normalized reduced area

Section 279 constructed the canonical orbit-degree normalization and Section 280 proved its exact
one-sheet formula on every refined quotient chart.  Before using that local formula for affine
Jacobian scaling, we close the global mass bookkeeping.  The normalization weight is everywhere
strictly positive and at most one, so `withDensity` cannot create infinite mass from a finite
quotient measure and cannot annihilate a nonzero quotient measure.  We then transport this statement
through the canonical regular-orbit homeomorphism and specialize it to the repaired bounded polygon
surface of Sections 265--273.
