/-! ## 402. Discharging metric redundancy from the planar `O(2)` source and extracting the transported angle

Section 401 left one local issue: a redundancy element fixing the listed vertex must transport an
interior active-sector point according to the same angular convention as `sectorAct`.  Before
attacking that one-dimensional angular statement, we remove a separate source-interface gap.
`EuclideanRedundancyData` from Section 397 is not retained as an independent hypothesis: for an
ordinary rational polygon it follows from two pieces already intrinsic to the standard reflection
model: the affine developing identity of Section 144 and the fact that the directional group acts by
Euclidean linear isometries, i.e. lies in `O(2)`.

The same argument also gives the exact centred affine formula at a polygon vertex.  Consequently the
image of every sufficiently small sector point has the same radius and lies in the target corrected
wedge.  We therefore obtain a unique *transported source angle*.  After this section the remaining
interior-covariance problem is purely one-dimensional: identify that transported angle with the
active angular coordinate prescribed by `sectorAct`.
-/

namespace OrthogonalRedundancySourceExactification

open FullCopyUnfolding
open PlanarRedundancyDevelopingData
open ConcreteFiniteEuclideanPolygonSource
