/-! ## 409. Closed-sector kernel reduction: strict interior versus the two genuine seams

Section 408 closed the actual converse kernel whenever both angular parameters are strict interior
points.  The only remaining local identifications are therefore the two closed-sector endpoints.
This section makes that statement precise at the final separation radius.  First we identify the
abstract one-sector angle with the genuine polygon interior angle on a raw representative.  We then
repackage the already-proved primitive and least-period seams at the refined radius.  Finally we use
one elementary but important observation: a strict interior point lies on no polygon side, whereas
an endpoint lies on an incident side.  Since redundancy preserves `onEdge`, equality in `Reduced`
can never mix those two kinds of points.

The final theorem therefore reduces *every* positive-radius closed-sector collision to exactly two
possibilities: the already-closed strict-interior kernel, or an endpoint--endpoint collision.  Thus
no new interior geometry remains; the next local step is purely the finite cyclic classification of
which endpoint pairs are the same seam (including the last-to-first seam).
-/

